**Theorem 77.3 (exact post-mark observation condition and sharpness).** For a fixed finite family of autonomous marked-field samples, let $O$ be its homogeneous observation matrix on the $W$ coordinates. Those samples with their known offsets and saved $z$ identify arbitrary original $x$ exactly if and only if $\operatorname{rank}O=q$. Moreover every terminating deterministic exact original-entry reporting protocol after this marking needs at least $q$ successful additional scalar Reads on every transcript, even if it may use adaptive original growth, known rational Adds and Ticks. Theorem 77.2 attains the bound.

**Proof.** For the fixed family, a full-column-rank matrix has a rational left inverse and solves for $h$. If rank is deficient, a nonzero rational $\eta\in\ker O$ gives two different $h,h+\eta$ with identical data, so the required original report cannot be unique.

For the adaptive claim freeze a terminating path at one $h$, fixing $t,\lambda_0,z$ and all its control choices. Original growth is a coordinate inclusion, Tick is linear and a fixed Add is a translation; hence every field and every Read reply is affine in $h$. If there are $R<q$ scalar Reads, the stacked homogeneous rows have a nonzero rational kernel vector $\eta$. Replacing $h$ by $h+\eta$ changes none of the replies along that path. Induction through the complete interface gives the same choices, operands, structural guards, bindings, frames and stopping decision, just as in Theorem 76.2. Yet the two owed original reports differ at an old $W$ coordinate. This contradicts exactness. $\square$

This is the minimum for original-entry reporting **after this actual marking with saved $z$**. It is not a minimum over all entrances, not a bound for physical restoration alone, and not §62's rank-$A$ minimum for an arbitrary current field. Proposition 58.3 still requires the original-terminal distinctions erased by marking to survive in $z$ or equivalent records. Sparse post-mark sampling does not pay that obligation twice or erase it.

